  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 241 AM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nFriday, our region is forecast to be mainly in a southwesterly \nupper- level flow with a developing trough in the Pacific \nNorthwest (PNW). Sunday, a second deep digging trough over the \nPacific Ocean will move inland. This will interact with the \nexisting trough over the PNW, but models diverge on how this \ninteraction will play out. The track and evolution of these \ntroughs will determine our precipitation chances heading into \nearly next week. \n\nCurrently, the first trough is forecast to become more of a cut\noff low sometime late Friday/early Saturday. This would favor \nmore widespread precipitation chances as it would generally keep\nour region in a southwest upper-level flow with various \nembedded shortwaves being ejected across the area Friday through\nat least Tuesday. Current Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) \nare around 20- 60% Friday through Monday, increasing west to \neast. Precipitation is expected to generally be showers. \nInstability is overall weak Friday with modest lapse rates and \nspotty CAPE values of <500 J/kg, so a weak thunderstorm or two \nisn't out of the question if it is able to tap into the weak \ninstability in the environment. \n\nMonday has the potential to be a windy day with gusts up to 50 \nmph possible for eastern Colorado. This will also depend on how \nthe upper-level troughs and precipitation chances pan out, but \nsomething to keep an eye on. \n\nTemperature wise, Friday is expected to be pleasant with high \ntemperatures forecast in the 60s to 70s. Saturday through \nMonday, we cool slightly with highs in the 60s. Overnight Monday\ninto Tuesday, temperatures could approach freezing across our \nColorado counties, with the potential for our first snow of the \nseason. Confidence remains low overall due to uncertainty in the\nweekend forecast, especially given that it is 7 days out.",
